  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-09T07:13:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "0e510168-c302-470a-9c81-660d72929671",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 090713\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion \nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV \n1214 AM PDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Well above-normal temperatures will persist through this \n  afternoon, with dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* Chances for gusty winds, cooler temperatures, and potentially \n  widespread precipitation arrive late Saturday through through \n  Tuesday. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through the middle of next week.\n\nHigh pressure remains in place through this afternoon before \nweakening ahead of a trough that digs over the Pacific Northwest. \nThis will lead to one more day of Major Heat Risk in Death Valley, \nwhere an Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Friday \nevening. Elsewhere, minor heat risk is expected, with a few areas of \nmoderate risk across the Colorado River Valley and lower desert \nelevations. Minor Heat Risk for most areas Saturday, but that will \nlower into the Little to No Risk category by Sunday as much cooler \ntemperatures are expected. \n\nOtherwise, we continue to monitor the phasing of tropical storm \nRachel with an incoming trough out of the Pacific Northwest. \nOverall, there have generally been some minor changes in the track \nover the past 12 hours as it pulls northeast into the region and it \nstill looks like the greatest threat of widespread precipitation \nwill be across western Arizona east through Lincoln, Clark, and \neastern San Bernardino Counties. The models have remained fairly \nconsistent that areas across Mohave County could see the highest \nprecipitation amounts, generally ranging from 1.00-2.00 inches \nstarting late Saturday night and continuing through Sunday night \nbefore decreasing. Areas to the east across southern Nevada and \neastern San Bernardino County have the potential of seeing 0.50-1.00 \ninch by Monday morning, with over an inch possible in the Spring \nMountains. Again, any slight shift in the track of Rachel and the \ndeepening west coast trough could bring changes to the overall \nprecipitation amounts. Shower and thunderstorm chances look to \npersist through Tuesday as a closed low tracks across southern \nNevada. This will bring snow levels down to around 9,000 feet, so it \nis quite possible that the Spring Mountains could see a little snow \non the peaks. In addition, there will also be a concern for stronger \nsurface winds near the remnant circulation as it moves across the \nlower Colorado River Valley and western Arizona, where some wind \ngusts of 40-50 mph or greater will be possible. A Wind Advisory has \nbeen issued for portions of Inyo County in California, and Nye and \nLincoln counties in Nevada starting Saturday afternoon and \ncontinuing through Sunday night. It is likely that additional wind \nadvisories will be needed later in the weekend. \n\nIn addition, much cooler temperatures will follow for Monday and \nTuesday as the low moves through, with heights falling below 560dm. \nHeights will slowly climb later in the week as high pressure \nrebuilds and temperatures return closer to normal. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Mostly \nclear skies last through the TAF period. Expect light southwest \nwinds to turn variable into the early morning. Winds stay light but \nturn more easterly by the late morning. Gusts and more moderate \nwinds arrive in the mid afternoon as winds turn more southeast. \nGusts cut off into the evening as winds reduce and turn \nsouthwesterly. These winds become light and variable into late \nFriday night. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Mostly clear skies last \nthrough the TAF period. Expect light winds through the night and \ninto tomorrow afternoon across the region. Breezy to gusty winds \naffect KDAG and KIFP into Friday afternoon, with winds easing at \nKIFP into the early evening while gusts continue at KDAG beyond the \nTAF period. Other TAF sites remain under 10 kts through the TAF \nperiod.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE....Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas      97(2024)*\nBishop         93(2015)\nNeedles       104(1996)*\nDaggett       103(1980)\nKingman        98(1910)\nDesert Rock    97(1996)\nDeath Valley  112(1996)\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     72(2024)*\nBishop        61(2025)\nNeedles       76(1988)*\nDaggett       69(1996)*\nKingman       68(1926)*\nDesert Rock   65(1991)*\nDeath Valley  78(2024)*\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
